1)  File System Space Management Algorithm
   BEGIN
    SET threshold = 80%
    SET critical_threshold = 90%
    WHILE system is running DO
        usage = GET_DISK_USAGE()
        IF usage > threshold THEN
            DISPLAY "Warning: Disk usage is above 80%"
            IDENTIFY all log_files
            SORT log_files BY size DESCENDING
            FOR each file IN log_files DO
                IF file is old THEN
                    DELETE file
                    DISPLAY "Old log file delete 
                ELSE
                    COMPRESS file
                    DISPLAY "Log file compressed"
                ENDIF
                usage = GET_DISK_USAGE()
                IF usage <= threshold THEN
                    DISPLAY "Disk usage is back to normal"
                    BREAK
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
            IF usage > critical_threshold THEN
                DISPLAY "CRITICAL: Disk space is almost full"
                SEND ALERT to system administrator
            ENDIF
        ELSE
            DISPLAY "Disk usage is normal"
        ENDIF
END
Keep checking how much disk space is being used.If it goes above 80%, take action.
Find the biggest and oldest log files.Delete, compress, or rotate unnecessary log files.
This helps prevent the disk from becoming full.

2)Priority-Based CPU Scheduling
BEGIN
INPUT process_list
    FOR each process IN process_list DO
        SET process status = READY
        RECORD process priority
        RECORD process burst_time
    ENDFOR
    SORT process_list BY priority (highest first)
        SELECT process WITH highest priority
        IF process is READY THEN
            ALLOCATE CPU time to selected process
            EXECUTE process for a fixed time slice
            REDUCE remaining burst_time
            IF process is completed THEN4
                DISPLAY "Process completed"
            ELSE
                REDUCE priority slightly
                INCREASE priority of waiting processes
                ADD process back to process_list
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
        SORT process_list BY priority (highest first)
    ENDWHILE
    DISPLAY "All processes completed"
    DISPLAY "CPU scheduling finished"
Check all the processes waiting for the CPU.Give the CPU first to the high-priority process.
Run the process for some CPU time.If it is not finished, put it back in the waiting list.
This helps the CPU work efficiently and avoids long waiting.

3)RAM Resource Management for Virtual Machines
BEGIN
    SET total_RAM = 16GB
    SET host_reserved = 4GB
    available_RAM = total_RAM - host_reserved  INPUT number_of_VMs
 SET min_RAM = 2GB
  FOR i = 1 TO number_of_VMs DO
   IF available_RAM >= min_RAM THEN
            ALLOCATE min_RAM to VM[i]
            available_RAM = available_RAM - min_RAM
            DISPLAY "RAM allocated to VM", i
            DISPLAY "Remaining RAM =", available_RAM
        ELSE
            DISPLAY "Not enough RAM for VM", i
            DISPLAY "VM cannot be started"
        ENDIF
    ENDFOR
    IF available_RAM > 0 THEN
        DISTRIBUTE remaining RAM to VMs
        DISPLAY "RAM distribution completed"
   ELSE
        DISPLAY "All available RAM has been allocated"
END
Start with 16 GB of total RAM.Keep 4 GB for the main/host system.
Give each VM at least 2 GB RAM.Check how much RAM is still available.
This helps all VMs run smoothly without using too much RAM.

